 "id": "abc",
 "title": "abc conjecture",
 "statement": {
  "text": "The abc conjecture is a conjecture in number theory that arose out of a discussion of Joseph Oesterlé and David Masser in 1985. It is stated in terms of three positive integers  and  that are relatively prime and satisfy . The conjecture essentially states that the product of the distinct prime factors of  cannot often be much smaller than . A number of famous conjectures and theorems in number theory would follow immediately from the abc conjecture or its versions. Mathematician Dorian Goldfeld described the abc conjecture as \"The most important unsolved problem in Diophantine analysis\".",
  "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abc_conjecture",
  "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
